<Layout
  title={`${title} | Treadmill Reviews USA`}
  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: "2026-11-19",
    modifiedTime: "2026-11-28",
    author: "Shaheer Ali Khan",
    section: "Fitness Equipment",
    tags: [
      "treadmill walking for diabetes",
      "walking after meals blood sugar",
      "type 2 diabetes exercise",
      "post-meal walk",
      "diabetes treadmill workout",
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
  stickyCta={{ text: "See When to Walk", link: "#timing" }}
>
  <article class="ed-article" data-template="editorial">
    <div class="ed-col">
      <nav class="ed-crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
        <a href="/training/">Workouts & Training</a>
        <span aria-hidden="true">›</span>
        <span>Training</span>
      </nav>
      <h1 class="ed-title">Treadmill Walking for Diabetes</h1>
      <p class="ed-dek">
        A short walk soon after a meal does more for blood sugar than the same walk at another time of day. For most people with diabetes that means 10 to 15 minutes at a brisk but comfortable pace, about 3 mph, after meals, adding up to at least 150 minutes a week.
      </p>

      <!-- Byline -->
      <div class="ed-byline">
        <img src="/authors/shaheer-ali-khan.png" alt="Shaheer Ali Khan, PhD" width="44" height="44" />
        <div class="ed-byline-text">
          By <a href="/editorial-standards/#who" rel="author">Shaheer Ali Khan, PhD</a><br />
          Updated November 28, 2026 · Exercise Physiology & Biomechanics Specialist · ACSM-EP, CSCS<br />
          <span class="ed-method">Based on published exercise guidance. General information, not medical advice.</span>
        </div>
        <a href="#timing" class="btn-ghost">When to Walk</a>
      </div>
      <p class="ed-disclosure">
        Nothing on this page is a paid placement, and it carries no affiliate links. <a href="/editorial-standards/">How we review.</a>
      </p>

      <div class="ed-body prose prose-lg">
        <p class="ed-lead">
          Walking is the exercise most people with diabetes are told to do, and a treadmill removes most
          of the reasons it does not happen: the weather, the dark, uneven pavements that are a worry
          with numb feet, and the distance from a bathroom. It also shows you your speed and time,
          which makes a walk easy to repeat and easy to compare with your glucose readings. This page
          covers what the research says about when to walk, how fast and for how long, how to avoid
          low blood sugar if you take insulin or certain tablets, and what to do about your feet. It
          is general guidance about using a treadmill, not medical advice: your diabetes team knows
          your medicines and your numbers, and they should have the last word on your plan.
        </p>

        <nav class="ed-toc not-prose" aria-label="On this page">
          <h2>On this page</h2>
          <ol>
            <li><a href="#short-answer">The Short Answer</a></li>
            <li><a href="#why-walking-helps">Why Walking Lowers Blood Sugar</a></li>
            <li><a href="#timing">When to Walk: Why After Meals Works Best</a></li>
            <li><a href="#speed">How Fast and How Long to Walk</a></li>
            <li><a href="#hypoglycemia">Avoiding Low Blood Sugar on the Treadmill</a></li>
            <li><a href="#feet">Protecting Your Feet</a></li>
            <li><a href="#other-cautions">Eyes, Heart and Blood Pressure: Other Things to Check</a></li>
            <li><a href="#four-week-start">A Four-Week Starting Plan</a></li>
            <li><a href="#faq">Frequently asked questions</a></li>
          </ol>
        </nav>

        <div id="short-answer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>The Short Answer</h2>
<p>
            <strong>For most people with type 2 diabetes, the most useful treadmill habit is a
            10 to 15-minute walk starting within about half an hour of finishing a meal, at a brisk
            pace of around 3 mph, building to at least 150 minutes of walking spread across the
            week.</strong> Walking after meals blunts the rise in blood sugar that follows eating, and
            regular walking improves how your body handles glucose for a day or more afterwards.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>When:</strong> after meals, especially the largest one, which for many people
            is the evening meal. Starting 15 to 30 minutes after you finish eating is a sensible
            default.</li>
            <li><strong>How long:</strong> 10 to 15 minutes per meal is enough to matter. Even 2 to
            5 minutes of easy walking helps compared with sitting.</li>
            <li><strong>How fast:</strong> a pace that makes you breathe a little faster but still lets
            you talk in full sentences. For many adults that is 2.5 to 3.5 mph on a flat belt.</li>
            <li><strong>How often:</strong> most days. The American Diabetes Association advises not
            going more than two days in a row without activity.</li>
            <li><strong>Safety:</strong> if you use insulin or a sulfonylurea, check your glucose
            before you walk and keep fast-acting carbohydrate within reach of the treadmill.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            Talk to your doctor or diabetes educator before you start if you take insulin or tablets
            that can cause lows, have diabetic eye disease, nerve damage in your feet, a foot ulcer, or
            any heart symptoms. If you are new to walking for exercise, our guide to <a href="/what-speed-should-i-walk-on-a-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">what speed to walk on a treadmill</a> explains how to find your own moderate pace. If your doctor asks for an exercise test before you build up, our guide to <a href="/how-much-does-a-treadmill-stress-test-cost/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill stress test costs</a> explains what the basic version and the imaging versions are likely to cost.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="why-walking-helps" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Why Walking Lowers Blood Sugar</h2>
<p>
            Working muscles take glucose out of the blood. During a walk, the large muscles of the legs
            and hips contract thousands of times, and those contractions move glucose into the muscle
            cells through a route that does not depend on insulin. That is why a walk lowers blood
            sugar even in people whose bodies respond poorly to insulin, and why the effect starts
            within minutes.
          </p>
          <p>
            There is a second, slower effect. After exercise, muscles stay more sensitive to insulin
            for a day or longer while they restock their own glucose stores. A walk today makes the
            same dose of insulin, whether your own or injected, work a little better tomorrow. Regular
            walking builds on this, which is why consistency matters more than any single session.
          </p>
          <p>
            Over months, the benefits reach beyond the day-to-day readings. Structured exercise
            programmes in people with type 2 diabetes typically lower HbA1c, the measure of average
            blood sugar over about three months, and they also improve blood pressure, blood fats and
            fitness, all of which matter because heart disease is the main long-term risk in
            diabetes. The American Diabetes Association&rsquo;s <em>Standards of Care</em> recommend
            physical activity for nearly everyone with diabetes for these reasons.
          </p>
          <p>
            A treadmill is not better than walking outdoors for any of this. Your muscles cannot tell
            the difference. Its advantage is practical: it is available at 9 pm in January, it is
            level, it keeps you near home, and it records exactly what you did, which matters when you
            are trying to link a walk to a glucose reading. If you are unsure whether the belt counts
            the same as the pavement, see <a href="/is-walking-on-a-treadmill-the-same-as-walking-outside/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">whether treadmill walking is the same as walking outside</a>.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="timing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>When to Walk: Why After Meals Works Best</h2>
<p>
            Blood sugar rises after a meal and usually peaks somewhere between 30 and 90 minutes after
            you start eating. A walk in that window puts working muscles to use at exactly the time
            there is the most glucose to clear, which is why post-meal walking keeps showing up in the
            research as the most efficient timing.
          </p>
          <p>
            Three studies are worth knowing about:
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>DiPietro and colleagues, <em>Diabetes Care</em>, 2013.</strong> Older adults
            with prediabetes did three 15-minute treadmill walks, one after each meal, or a single
            45-minute walk. The after-meal walks controlled 24-hour blood sugar about as well as the
            longer walk, and the walk after the evening meal had the largest effect on the
            after-dinner rise.</li>
            <li><strong>Reynolds and colleagues, <em>Diabetologia</em>, 2016.</strong> Adults with type
            2 diabetes walked either 10 minutes after each main meal or 30 minutes once a day. The
            total was the same, but the after-meal pattern lowered blood sugar after meals more,
            with the biggest difference after the evening meal, which is when people tend to eat the
            most carbohydrate and sit down afterwards.</li>
            <li><strong>Buffey and colleagues, <em>Sports Medicine</em>, 2022.</strong> A meta-analysis
            of studies that interrupted long periods of sitting found that short bouts of light
            walking, as little as 2 to 5 minutes, lowered the rise in blood sugar after eating
            compared with sitting still. Standing helped less than walking.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            Put together, the practical rule is simple: if you can only walk once a day, walk after the
            biggest meal; if you can walk more than once, walk after each meal, even briefly. A
            treadmill in the kitchen-to-sofa route of a house makes this far easier than getting
            dressed to go outside three times a day.
          </p>
          <div class="not-prose overflow-x-auto my-8"><table class="min-w-[520px] w-full text-sm border-collapse">
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Your day</th>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Suggested treadmill pattern</th>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Weekly total</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Home all day</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">10 to 15 min after each of three meals</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">210 to 315 min</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Office job, evenings free</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">20 to 30 min after the evening meal, plus a 5-min walk after lunch where you work</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">175 to 245 min</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Very busy</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">10 min after dinner on weekdays, 30 min on two weekend days</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">110 min (build up from here)</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Just starting</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">5 min after dinner at an easy pace, adding 1 to 2 min each week</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">35 min rising toward 150</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table></div>
          <p>
            <strong>Exercising before breakfast</strong> is a different case. Some people with type 2
            diabetes see their glucose rise during a fasted morning workout, because the hormones that
            wake you up also release stored glucose. That is not harmful, but it can be confusing. If
            your fasting readings behave oddly around morning walks, try walking after breakfast
            instead and compare.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="speed" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>How Fast and How Long to Walk</h2>
<p>
            The target is <strong>moderate intensity</strong>: you are breathing faster and you can
            feel your heart working, but you can still speak in full sentences. The US Centers for
            Disease Control and Prevention give brisk walking at about 3 mph or faster as an example
            of moderate activity. On a treadmill that is a setting, not a guess.
          </p>
          <p>
            Your own number may be lower or higher. A shorter, older or less fit person may reach
            moderate effort at 2.5 mph; a tall, fit walker may need 3.8 mph or a little incline. Use
            how it feels rather than the display:
          </p>
          <div class="not-prose overflow-x-auto my-8"><table class="min-w-[520px] w-full text-sm border-collapse">
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Effort</th>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Talk test</th>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Typical treadmill setting</th>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Use it for</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Easy</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Can sing or chat freely</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">1.5 to 2.5 mph, flat</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Short after-meal walks, warm-up, first weeks</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Moderate</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Can talk, not sing</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">2.5 to 3.5 mph, 0 to 3% incline</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Most sessions; counts toward 150 min</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Vigorous</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">A few words at a time</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">3.5 to 4 mph with 5% or more incline</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Only once moderate walking is routine and cleared</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table></div>
          <p>
            <strong>Weekly amount.</strong> The ADA&rsquo;s <em>Standards of Care</em> recommend at
            least 150 minutes a week of moderate-to-vigorous aerobic activity for most adults with
            diabetes, spread over at least three days, with no more than two days in a row without
            activity. They also recommend strength exercise two or three times a week, which a
            treadmill does not provide, so pair your walking with simple resistance work.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Breaking up sitting.</strong> The same standards advise interrupting long periods
            of sitting every 30 minutes. If you work from home, a short walk on a treadmill, or a <a href="/what-is-a-walking-pad/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">walking pad</a> under a standing desk, is one of the easiest ways to do it.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Incline instead of speed.</strong> If 3 mph feels too fast for your balance or
            joints, keep the belt slower and add 2 to 5% incline. That raises the effort without
            asking your feet to move faster, and it is often easier on knees and on feet with reduced
            feeling. Our guide to <a href="/what-incline-should-i-walk-on-a-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">what incline to walk on</a> explains how much difference each percent makes.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="hypoglycemia" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Avoiding Low Blood Sugar on the Treadmill</h2>
<p>
            If your diabetes is managed with diet, metformin, or most newer tablets and injections,
            walking is very unlikely to drop you too low on its own. The risk is real for people who
            use <strong>insulin</strong> or take a <strong>sulfonylurea</strong> such as gliclazide,
            glipizide or glimepiride, because those push blood sugar down whether or not you are
            exercising. Ask your diabetes team which group your medicines put you in.
          </p>
          <p>
            For people with type 1 diabetes, an international consensus statement led by Michael
            Riddell (<em>The Lancet Diabetes &amp; Endocrinology</em>, 2017) gives starting points that
            many clinics use as a reference:
          </p>
          <div class="not-prose overflow-x-auto my-8"><table class="min-w-[520px] w-full text-sm border-collapse">
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Glucose before walking</th>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Consensus suggestion (type 1 diabetes)</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Below 90 mg/dL (5.0 mmol/L)</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Take 10 to 20 g of glucose and delay exercise until levels are above 90 mg/dL</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">90 to 124 mg/dL (5.0 to 6.9 mmol/L)</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Take about 10 g of glucose before aerobic exercise</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">126 to 270 mg/dL (7.0 to 15.0 mmol/L)</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Aerobic exercise can usually start</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Above 270 mg/dL (15.0 mmol/L)</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Check ketones; do not exercise if they are raised</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table></div>
          <p>
            Those figures are a reference point, not a personal prescription. Your team may set
            different thresholds, especially if you use a pump or a closed-loop system, which can
            reduce insulin delivery before exercise.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">A treadmill routine that keeps lows manageable</h3>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Check before you start.</strong> A finger-stick or a glance at your sensor
            takes seconds and tells you whether to eat first.</li>
            <li><strong>Keep sugar on the console.</strong> Glucose tablets or a small juice box in
            the cup holder mean you never have to step off and leave the room to treat a low.</li>
            <li><strong>Know the signs.</strong> Shakiness, sweating more than the effort explains,
            sudden hunger, blurred vision or confusion mean stop the belt, sit down, check and treat.</li>
            <li><strong>Watch the evening.</strong> Insulin sensitivity stays raised for hours after
            exercise, so lows can come at night after an evening walk. Check before bed on days you
            have walked more than usual.</li>
            <li><strong>Use the safety key.</strong> Clip it on every time. If you feel faint, pulling
            the key stops the belt before you fall.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            Feeling light-headed when you step off the belt is common and is usually a blood-pressure
            effect rather than low blood sugar, but with diabetes you should check rather than
            assume. A two-minute slow cool-down before you stop helps with both.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="feet" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Protecting Your Feet</h2>
<p>
            Feet are the part of the body diabetes treats worst, and they are the part a treadmill
            uses most. Nerve damage (peripheral neuropathy) can mean you do not feel a blister forming,
            and reduced circulation means a small wound heals slowly. None of this means you should
            not walk. The ADA&rsquo;s 2016 position statement on physical activity (Colberg and
            colleagues, <em>Diabetes Care</em>) says people with peripheral neuropathy and no active
            foot ulcer can take part in moderate weight-bearing exercise, provided they wear proper
            footwear and check their feet daily.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Shoes that fit at the end of the day.</strong> Feet swell during the day and
            during a walk. Buy in the afternoon, with a thumb&rsquo;s width at the toe, and avoid
            walking in socks or barefoot on the belt. Our <a href="/best-walking-shoes-for-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill walking shoes guide</a> covers what to look for.</li>
            <li><strong>Seam-free, moisture-wicking socks.</strong> Damp cotton and seams rub. Diabetic
            socks are an option but not a requirement.</li>
            <li><strong>Look after every walk.</strong> Check soles, heels and between the toes, using
            a mirror if needed. Redness that lasts, a blister, a cut or a warm spot is a reason to
            stop walking on that foot and call your podiatrist or doctor.</li>
            <li><strong>Build time gradually.</strong> New friction points show up when you jump from
            10 minutes to 40. Add a few minutes a week.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            If you have an open ulcer or a foot that is hot, swollen and red, do not walk on it.
            Non-weight-bearing exercise such as a <a href="/best-recumbent-bikes/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>recumbent bike</a> or seated arm work keeps you active
            while it heals, and your foot team will tell you when walking is safe again.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="other-cautions" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Eyes, Heart and Blood Pressure: Other Things to Check</h2>
<p>
            Walking is gentle enough that most people with diabetes can start without special tests,
            but a few complications change what is sensible. Raise these with your doctor before
            building up:
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Diabetic retinopathy.</strong> With advanced (proliferative) retinopathy,
            vigorous effort, straining and steep incline work are usually discouraged because sudden
            rises in blood pressure can trigger bleeding in the eye. Steady moderate walking is
            generally fine, but ask your eye specialist.</li>
            <li><strong>Heart disease or symptoms.</strong> Chest pain, pressure, unusual breathlessness
            or palpitations on exertion need checking before you exercise, and diabetes can mute the
            warning signs of heart trouble. If your doctor wants to see how your heart responds,
            they may order a <a href="/treadmill-stress-test/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill stress test</a>.</li>
            <li><strong>Blood pressure medicines.</strong> Beta blockers blunt your heart rate
            response, so heart-rate targets on the console will read low. Use the talk test
            instead.</li>
            <li><strong>Autonomic neuropathy.</strong> This can affect heart rate and blood pressure
            control, making dizziness on stopping more likely. Long, gradual cool-downs help.</li>
            <li><strong>Kidney disease.</strong> Moderate walking is usually encouraged, but ask
            about fluids and intensity if your kidney function is reduced.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            Walking is also good for blood pressure and cholesterol, which is part of why the
            guidelines push it so hard. Diabetes care is as much about protecting the heart as it is
            about the glucose number.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="four-week-start" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>A Four-Week Starting Plan</h2>
<p>
            This plan assumes your doctor is happy for you to walk and that you are starting from
            little or no regular exercise. If you are already walking, start at the week that feels
            easy and carry on from there.
          </p>
          <div class="not-prose overflow-x-auto my-8"><table class="min-w-[520px] w-full text-sm border-collapse">
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Week</th>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">After-meal walks</th>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Speed and incline</th>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Weekly total</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">1</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">5 to 10 min after dinner, 5 days</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Easy, 1.5 to 2.5 mph, flat</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">25 to 50 min</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">2</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">10 min after dinner, plus 5 min after lunch, 5 days</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Easy to moderate, add 0.2 mph</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">75 min</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">3</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">15 min after dinner, 10 min after lunch, 5 days</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Moderate, about 2.5 to 3 mph</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">125 min</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">4</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">15 min after dinner, 10 min after lunch or breakfast, 6 days</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Moderate; try 1 to 3% incline</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">150 min</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table></div>
          <p>
            Every session starts with two minutes at an easy pace and ends with two minutes slowing
            down. Write down your glucose before and about an hour after the walk for the first few
            weeks, or look at the trace if you use a sensor; seeing the after-meal peak come down is
            the best motivation there is. For a longer progression once this feels routine, our <a href="/treadmill-walking-workouts/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill walking workouts</a> add variety, and our guide for older walkers on <a href="/treadmill-speed-for-seniors/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">safe treadmill speeds for seniors</a> covers balance if that is a concern.
          </p>
        </div>


        <!-- Common mistakes -->
        <div id="common-mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>What Goes Wrong With Treadmill Walking for Diabetes</h2>
          <p>Most of these come from treating the walk as separate from meals and medicines.</p>
          <ol class="ed-mistakes not-prose">
            <li>
              <h3>Walking at the wrong end of the day</h3>
              <p>A single long walk at a time unrelated to food does less for blood sugar than the same minutes after meals. If you only have one slot, use it after your largest meal, which for most people is dinner.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Stepping on without checking, on insulin</h3>
              <p>If you use insulin or a sulfonylurea, a walk started at a low-normal reading can drop you into a hypo partway through. Check first, eat if your team says to at your level, and keep glucose tablets in the cup holder.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Ignoring your feet until they hurt</h3>
              <p>With reduced feeling, pain is a late warning. Wear shoes that fit, avoid walking in socks or barefoot, and look at your feet after every walk. A blister or red patch means rest that foot and call your podiatrist.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Going from nothing to an hour</h3>
              <p>A big jump in walking time raises the risk of blisters, sore joints, and lows if you take insulin. Start with 5 to 10 minutes after one meal, add a few minutes a week, and let your team adjust medicines as you go.</p>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </div>

        <!-- The bottom line -->
        <div id="bottom-line" class="ed-bottomline">
          <h2>The takeaway</h2>
          <p>
            <strong class="text-white">Walk 10 to 15 minutes after meals at a brisk but conversational
            pace, about 3 mph for many people, and build to at least 150 minutes a week.</strong> If you
            can only walk once a day, walk after your biggest meal.
          </p>
          <p>
            Check your glucose before walking if you use insulin or a sulfonylurea, keep sugar on the
            console, wear proper shoes and look at your feet afterwards. Start small, and ask your
            diabetes team to review your plan and your medicines as your walking increases. If knee
            pain is what stops you, our <a href="/treadmill-walking-with-arthritis/" class="text-[#5AA9FF] font-bold no-underline">arthritis walking guide</a> can help.
          </p>
        </div>

