        <!-- FAQ -->
        <section id="faq" class="ed-faq scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
          <details open>
            <summary><h3>Is walking on a treadmill good for diabetes?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Yes. Walking moves glucose into working muscles and makes the body more sensitive to insulin for a day or more afterwards. A treadmill makes it easier to walk at the most useful time, soon after meals, whatever the weather, and lets you repeat the same speed and time so you can see the effect on your readings.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>How long should a diabetic walk after eating?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              About 10 to 15 minutes, starting within roughly 15 to 30 minutes of finishing the meal, is enough to lower the after-meal rise in blood sugar. Studies have found benefits from as little as 2 to 5 minutes of light walking compared with sitting, so a short walk is far better than none.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>What speed should a diabetic walk on a treadmill?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Aim for moderate effort: breathing faster but still able to talk in full sentences. For many adults that is about 2.5 to 3.5 mph on a flat belt. If faster walking is hard on your balance or joints, keep the speed lower and add 2 to 5% incline instead.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>How much does walking lower blood sugar?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              It varies with the person, the meal, the medicines and the walk. In a 2016 Diabetologia study of people with type 2 diabetes, walking for 10 minutes after each meal lowered after-meal glucose more than one 30-minute daily walk, especially after the evening meal. Your own before-and-after readings are the best guide.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Can walking cause low blood sugar in diabetics?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              It can if you take insulin or a sulfonylurea such as glipizide, glimepiride or gliclazide. Check your glucose before walking, keep fast-acting carbohydrate on the treadmill console, and check again before bed after a longer walk, because lows can come hours later. With most other diabetes medicines the risk is low.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Is it better to walk before or after eating with diabetes?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              After eating, for blood sugar control. A walk soon after a meal uses glucose when there is the most of it in the blood. Walking before breakfast can be fine, but some people with type 2 diabetes see a rise during fasted morning exercise, which is normal but can be confusing.
            </p>
          </details>
        </section>

